## IMPORTANT RULES
Extract business rules at the smallest executable unit. If a sentence contains multiple retrievals, mappings, filters, calculations, comparisons, sorting operations, ranking operations, or updates, create separate YAML rules for each operation. Never merge multiple business operations into one rule.
2. Preserve entity names, attribute names, business terminology, and formulas exactly as  written in the source document.

3. Do not replace business logic with generic statements such as:
      attribute exists
      record exists
      value exists
   Instead, capture the complete business condition as specified.

4. Capture complete lookup criteria, including all mapping attributes when multiple attributes are used.
5. Capture complete filter criteria exactly as specified.
6. Capture complete retrieval attributes. If multiple attributes are retrieved, list all of them.
7. Capture complete computation formulas without modifying or simplifying the expression.

8. Every extracted business rule must belong to exactly one section:
      Eligibility Check – rules related to retrieval, lookup, mapping, validation,  filtering, joins, record identification, and eligibility.
      Computation Rules – rules related to calculations, updates, lookups supporting computation, data migration and trigger.
9. If a single business statement contains both eligibility and computation logic, split it into separate rules:
     One rule under Eligibility Check
     One rule under Computation Rules
10. Create a separate YAML rule for every independent business rule. Do not combine unrelated rules into a single entry.
11. Every computation rule must use exactly one of the following operation types:
    calculation
    update
    migration
    lookup
    trigger
12. Classify computation rules as follows:
    Formula-based or derived value logic → calculation
    Default value assignments, null handling, or attribute modifications → update
    Insert, Upsert, Copy, Populate, or data movement operations → migration
    Lookups performed solely to support a computation or update → lookup
13. If multiple business conditions apply to a single computation rule, combine them within the condition field rather than creating duplicate computation rules.
14. se YAML lists for fields containing multiple values, such as retrieve and source_attributes.
15. Do not omit any business information. Always capture:
    source entities
    target entities
    target attributes
    lookup criteria
    filter criteria
    conditions
    retrieved attributes
    formulas
    mappings
16. If a required YAML field is not specified in the BRD/TBRD, assign it the value null. Do not invent missing information.
17. Generate sequential rule identifiers:
    Eligibility rules: E001, E002, E003, ...
    Computation rules: C001, C002, C003, ...
18. The Eligibility Check section must contain sufficient information to enable downstream SQL query generation.
19. The Computation Rules section must contain sufficient information to enable downstream test scenario generation.

20. Every explicit business action must become an independent YAML rule, including retrieval, filtering, sorting, grouping, counting, ranking, comparison, selection, validation, conditional branching, triggering downstream services, updates, and migrations. Do not omit intermediate business operations.

21. Sorting, ordering, ranking, TOP-N selection, MAX, MIN, GROUP BY, COUNT, DISTINCT, aggregation, and comparison operations are mandatory business rules and must always be extracted explicitly.

22. Do not omit temporal business conditions. Date ranges, effective dates, current year, rolling windows, recent years, build years, historical periods, and time-based filters must be preserved exactly as written.

23. Extract every IF, ELSE, OTHERWISE, FAIL, SUCCESS, FALLBACK, NEXT SERVICE, TRIGGER, or alternate processing path as independent computation rules. Never omit fallback logic.

24. Business comparisons must be preserved exactly. Comparisons include greater than, less than, equal, highest, lowest, adjacent, same, different, matching, non-matching, maximum, minimum, ranking, and precedence logic.

24. Copy business conditions verbatim. Never replace them with simplified expressions such as "record exists", "value exists", or "attribute exists".

25. Treat the document as an executable workflow. Every numbered step, sub-step, bullet point, or sequential action represents one or more business rules. Preserve execution order exactly as described.

26. Do not summarize, explain, interpret, generate SQL, generate test cases, or add commentary.

27. Return only valid YAML following the specified output schema. Do not output Markdown, code fences, or any additional text outside the YAML.


